# 🔟 Insert into Heap

When inserting an element:

### Step 1

Add the element at the end of the array.

### Step 2

Compare it with its parent.

### Step 3

If the heap property is violated, swap them.

### Step 4

Continue moving upward.

This process is called:

```text
Heapify Up
```

or:

```text
Bubble Up
```

---

# 1️⃣1️⃣ Insert in Min Heap

Suppose we have:

```text
[10, 20, 15, 30, 40]
```

Insert:

```text
5
```

First:

```text
[10, 20, 15, 30, 40, 5]
```

Parent of `5`:

```text
15
```

Since:

```text
5 < 15
```

Swap:

```text
[10, 20, 5, 30, 40, 15]
```

Now compare `5` with parent `10`.

```text
5 < 10
```

Swap:

```text
[5, 20, 10, 30, 40, 15]
```

Heap restored.

---

# 1️⃣2️⃣ Min Heap Insertion

### C++ Code

```cpp
void insertMinHeap(
    vector<int>& heap,
    int value)
{
    heap.push_back(value);

    int index = heap.size() - 1;

    while(index > 0)
    {
        int parent = (index - 1) / 2;

        if(heap[parent] <= heap[index])
            break;

        swap(heap[parent], heap[index]);

        index = parent;
    }
}
```

### Complexity

```text
Time: O(log n)

Space: O(1)
```

---

# 1️⃣3️⃣ Insert in Max Heap

For Max Heap, we move the newly inserted element upward while:

```text
Child > Parent
```

### C++ Code

```cpp
void insertMaxHeap(
    vector<int>& heap,
    int value)
{
    heap.push_back(value);

    int index = heap.size() - 1;

    while(index > 0)
    {
        int parent = (index - 1) / 2;

        if(heap[parent] >= heap[index])
            break;

        swap(heap[parent], heap[index]);

        index = parent;
    }
}
```

---

# 1️⃣4️⃣ Delete Root from Heap

The root is the most important element:

```text
Min Heap → Minimum

Max Heap → Maximum
```

To delete the root:

### Step 1

Replace the root with the last element.

### Step 2

Remove the last element.

### Step 3

Compare the new root with its children.

### Step 4

Swap with the appropriate child.

### Step 5

Continue until the heap property is restored.

This process is called:

```text
Heapify Down
```

or:

```text
Bubble Down
```

---

# 1️⃣5️⃣ Delete Root from Min Heap

Example:

```text
        10
       /  \
      20   15
     / \   /
    30 40 25
```

Array:

```text
[10, 20, 15, 30, 40, 25]
```

Delete root `10`.

Move last element:

```text
[25, 20, 15, 30, 40]
```

Compare `25` with children:

```text
20 and 15
```

Choose the smaller child:

```text
15
```

Swap:

```text
[15, 20, 25, 30, 40]
```

Heap restored.

---

# 1️⃣6️⃣ Min Heapify Down

### C++ Code

```cpp
void heapifyDown(
    vector<int>& heap,
    int index)
{
    int n = heap.size();

    while(true)
    {
        int left = 2 * index + 1;
        int right = 2 * index + 2;

        int smallest = index;

        if(left < n &&
           heap[left] < heap[smallest])
        {
            smallest = left;
        }

        if(right < n &&
           heap[right] < heap[smallest])
        {
            smallest = right;
        }

        if(smallest == index)
            break;

        swap(heap[index], heap[smallest]);

        index = smallest;
    }
}
```

---

# 1️⃣7️⃣ Delete from Min Heap

```cpp
int deleteMin(vector<int>& heap)
{
    if(heap.empty())
        return -1;

    int answer = heap[0];

    heap[0] = heap.back();

    heap.pop_back();

    if(!heap.empty())
        heapifyDown(heap, 0);

    return answer;
}
```

### Complexity

```text
Time: O(log n)

Space: O(1)
```

